#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171794 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172005 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "747b25be-ff4e-5b1e-abcc-5edd1edd9790" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Situations That Can Be Modeled Using an Exponential Distribution"}] :question/id #uuid "fa51ca96-4ed2-585b-88e6-244b4d8dd66d" :question/math-academy-id "e-16588" :question/problem "Which of the following random variables could be modeled by an exponential distribution?\n\nI. The time interval between successive arrivals of customers in a store\n\nII. The number of customers in a store at a particular time\n\nIII. The weight of a child at birth" :question/worked-solution "The exponential distribution can be thought of as the continuous analog of the geometric distribution. It models the amount of continuous time or space until a particular event occurs, where the events should occur independently and at a constant average rate.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I could be modeled using an exponential distribution. Customer arrivals can be modeled as independent events with a constant average rate, and we wish to know the amount of time until the next customer enters the store.\n\n- Random variable II does *not* follow an exponential distribution. There is no interval of continuous time or space. The number of customers in a store is discrete, not continuous.\n\n- Random variable III does *not* follow an exponential distribution. There are no events that occur at a constant average rate.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171795 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172006 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fb820969-b00e-58fd-9e6d-f8c786f91b33" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Probabilities With Exponential Random Variables on a Bounded Interval"}] :question/id #uuid "4c677102-9f65-58f2-8998-8353f81d84d8" :question/math-academy-id "e-16589" :question/problem "At an electronics store, smartphone purchases occur continuously in time, independently, and at a constant average rate of one sale every $20$ minutes. Rounded to four decimal places, what is the probability that the next smartphone is sold within the next $5$ minutes?" :question/worked-solution "Since the purchases occur continuously in time, independently, and at a constant average rate, we can model the time until the next purchase as an exponential distribution.\n\nAn exponential random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda$ has the following probability density function:\n\n![](images/71/714585d89ba3f1d529cd6b64658b778eafa8c346ff9ec592d4997e56803b913e.svg)\n\nLet $X$ be the amount of time until the next smartphone is purchased. Since there is a purchase every $20$ minutes on average, the rate is $\\lambda = 1/20$ purchases per minute.\n\nThe time intervals between successive purchases can be modeled as an exponential random variable with rate $\\lambda = 1/20,$ and we can write \n\n$$\nX\\sim \\textrm{Exp} \\left( \\dfrac{1}{20} \\right).\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n![](images/9f/9f4bcc34982b48061f0fda3c3136e97f37030cb1cf7d526919860ec53274c644.svg)\n\nTherefore, the probability that a customer will purchase a smartphone within the next $5$ minutes is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<5) & ={\\int }_{0}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{5}\\frac{1}{20}{e}^{-x/20}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={-{e}^{-x/20}|}_{0}^{5} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-1/4}+{e}^{0} \\\\  & =1-{e}^{-1/4} \\\\  & ≈0.2212\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171796 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172007 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "faf45d2f-484a-56ee-af3e-c14a63721c19" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Probabilities With Exponential Random Variables on a Unbounded Interval"}] :question/id #uuid "4baa7bca-acfb-5f9b-a6b4-1fc4b949555d" :question/math-academy-id "e-16590" :question/problem "At a clothing store, customers purchase clothes for newborns continuously in time, independently, and at a constant average rate of one sale every $30$ minutes. What is the probability that it takes more than $60$ minutes to sell the next set of newborn clothes?" :question/worked-solution "Since the purchases occur continuously in time, independently, and at a constant average rate, we can model the time until the next purchase as an exponential distribution.\n\nAn exponential random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda$ has the following probability density function:\n\n![](images/6a/6a82ddd5e5aa9cc641d3720adb9b998c9bc7e72c4e6edfb929d413a6fc584f87.svg)\n\nLet $X$ be the amount of time until the next set of newborn clothes is sold. Since there is a purchase every $30$ minutes on average, the rate is $\\lambda =1/30$ purchases per minute.\n\nThe time intervals between successive purchases can be modeled as an exponential random variable with rate $\\lambda =1/30,$ and we can write \n\n$$\nX\\sim \\textrm{Exp} \\left( \\dfrac{1}{30} \\right).\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n![](images/79/7960b1171c6697fe49628a8f49ac07671d05acf323ebdfb9734175e12a605640.svg)\n\nTherefore, the probability that it takes more than $60$ minutes to sell the next set of newborn clothes is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 60) & ={\\int }_{60}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{60}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{30}{e}^{-x/30}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={-{e}^{-x/30}|}_{60}^{\\infty } \\\\  & =-{e}^{-\\infty }+{e}^{-2} \\\\  & =0+{e}^{-2} \\\\  & ={e}^{-2} \\\\  & ≈0.1353.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171797 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172008 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e40fd627-b52a-5d60-b144-2087979533fe" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Probabilities Using the Memoryless Property"}] :question/id #uuid "821b7a41-c7ee-54fc-9b12-b13330b109ef" :question/math-academy-id "e-16591" :question/problem "A malfunctioning internet server approves connection requests continuously in time, independently, and at a constant average rate of $2$ requests per minute. If a user has been waiting for $30$ seconds to connect, what is the probability that they will have to wait at least $20$ more seconds?" :question/worked-solution "Since connection request approval occurs continuously, independently, and at a constant average rate, we can model the time until the next request is approved using an exponential distribution.\n\nLet $X$ be the user's waiting time in seconds. Since the server approves $2$ requests per minute on average (or $1$ request every $30$ seconds), the rate is $\\lambda = \\dfrac{1}{30}$ requests per second, and we can write\n\n$$\nX\\sim \\textrm{Exp} \\left( \\dfrac{1}{30} \\right).\n$$\n\nRecall that exponential random variables have the memoryless property. So, given two positive real numbers $s, t,$ we have that\n\n$$\nP(X>s+t \\, \\big|\\, X>s) = P(X > t) = e^{-\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda = \\dfrac{1}{30},$ $s=30,$ and $t=20.$ \n\nSubstituting these values into the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>30+20\\,|\\,X>30) & =P(X>20) \\\\  & ={e}^{-\\lambda t} \\\\  & ={e}^{-(1/30)\\cdot (20)} \\\\  & ={e}^{-2/3} \\\\  & ≈0.513.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434466 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172005 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "747b25be-ff4e-5b1e-abcc-5edd1edd9790" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Situations That Can Be Modeled Using an Exponential Distribution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1916b91d-3dd2-5b7d-8065-6c9e62cb7944" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434451} {:answer/id #uuid "48c12e76-6dfc-5fcc-8a40-a983537294e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434453} {:answer/id #uuid "4a18a4a3-0167-535b-9b6b-71a